{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Mohsen Heidari, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Useful terminology for separating fixed-design/path-known regret from sequential or universal regret, matching the run's quantifier issues.","title":"Sequential vs. Fixed Design Regrets in Online Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISIT50566.2022.9834776","venue_year":"IEEE ISIT 2022"},{"authors":"Angelos Assos, Idan Attias, Yuval Dagan, Constantinos Daskalakis, Maxwell K. Fishelson","relevance":"Potential background for recasting the fixed-envelope criterion as an infinite zero-sum game or best-response-oracle feasibility problem.","title":"Online Learning and Solving Infinite Games with an ERM Oracle","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/assos23a.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are characterized by switching or drought-complexity parameters.","title":"Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Vasilis Kontonis, Mingchen Ma, Christos Tzamos","relevance":"Fixed-design online learning with order effects is adjacent to the path-known, horizon-oblivious regime where one sequence must serve many prefixes.","title":"The Gain from Ordering in Online Learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/c9b1fe9c41f1eeec3a659154d575a282-Abstract-Conference.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2023"},{"authors":"Alexander Rakhlin, Karthik Sridharan","relevance":"Predictable-sequence methods are relevant background for paths where delayed fills are predictable or stable-high/stable-low policies nearly match the comparator.","title":"Online Learning with Predictable Sequences","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v30/Rakhlin13.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2013"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The claimed formula may fail in edge cases such as M=1, S=0, h<2 ell, or because the proposed primal mixing ignores an active horizon. Strict crossing also needs eta-loss bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","finite LP regression"],"summary":"Audit the solver's single-drought damping lemma for the two-level formal left-limit path h^M, 0^N, ell, h. Write a complete primal marginal construction and matching dual lambda certificate for D_{M+N+2}=S(1-ell/h), where S=[ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+]_+. Check all boundary cases and then add the strict-crossing implementation.","title":"Audit Single-Drought Damping","why_promising":"This is the newest concrete claimed progress. If correct, it gives an exact local mechanism explaining how old high prices damp later low/high conflicts; if false, finding the failure will immediately refine the two-level program."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Different droughts may interact through shared early quote marginals, and local S_i certificates may neither add nor localize. A path may need nonstable switching policies not captured by stable high or stable low quotes.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dichotomy theorem","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try to decompose arbitrary two-level paths into high-free drought intervals between h occurrences. Define a surplus or queue process related to Q_T=[ell(L_ell(T)-1)-h(L_h(T)-1)]_+. Prove a dichotomy: either Q_T=o(T) and the stable h quote gives o(T) regret, or some comparable-scale prefix has D_n=Omega(n). Treat the case where h eventually stops separately via a stable ell quote.","title":"Two-Level Drought Decomposition","why_promising":"The solver's lemma and stable-high corollary point to a concrete potential function. A successful dichotomy would settle the two-level finite-alphabet localization problem, the current main sandbox."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the fixed-envelope criterion. The hard part is converting repeated augmented certificates into ordinary D_n mass or a real global obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","LP duality","localization"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds or horizon weights beyond a prefix. It should reproduce the boundary defect in the separated-block example and the S term in h^M,0^N,ell,h. Then test whether repeated augmented defects force large ordinary D_n at comparable scales, or instead yield a separator from fixed-envelope feasibility.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Prefix LP","why_promising":"Iteration 14 refuted direct charging of boundary defects to shorter-prefix D_m because late shadow objectives are invisible. Keeping those shadows explicitly is the natural next localization object."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Simple separated blocks are known to be diluted, and fresh quote opportunities may make every proposed separator learnable. Numerical evidence alone would not prove a separator.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite LP experiment","construction"],"summary":"Search for a genuine market path with D_n=o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope by chaining many damped two-level droughts. Tune M_i,N_i so each local conflict is damped relative to total prefix length, while the horizon-optimal quote keeps moving enough to defeat any fixed marginal sequence. Verify candidates against the exact finite-prefix LP, not only stable-quote regret.","title":"Damped-Drought Separator Search","why_promising":"If the gluing implication is false, the solver's damping lemma suggests the separator must exploit many individually small but projectively incompatible shadow conflicts rather than one large block."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The two-level positive-part simplification may not extend cleanly. Nested maxima over comparator levels and learner-side response levels can destroy uncrossing or total-unimodularity arguments.","suggested_lenses":["finite alphabet","proof strategy","recursive potential"],"summary":"Generalize two-level damping to finite alphabets c_1<...<c_k. Use the finite-alphabet dual with next-hit times N_j(t) and last-exceedance times L_j(T) to look for a recursive record-level surplus queue: higher stable comparators damp lower-level conflicts unless a lower-level surplus becomes visible at comparable scale.","title":"Recursive Finite-Alphabet Potential","why_promising":"The two-level case may be too special. A k-level potential would turn the finite-alphabet reduction into structural progress and could expose exactly where nested maxima block localization."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Numerical artifacts can mislead, especially with strict-crossing left limits and near-degenerate dual optima. Small n may miss asymptotic phenomena.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","dual extraction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Build an exact finite-prefix LP experiment harness for finite alphabets in the formal left-limit model. For two-level paths, compare D_n to the stable-high gap Q_T, single-drought S values, and sums of local drought surpluses. Extract dual supports for repeated droughts, alternating paths with h<2 ell, and random interleavings.","title":"Finite-Prefix LP Harness","why_promising":"The current frontier is pattern discovery inside a concrete finite LP. A reliable experiment suite would quickly falsify overstrong decomposition claims and produce dual certificates for explorers to prove."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"External infinite-game and fixed-design results may only repackage compactness or require assumptions absent from market suffix maxima. They should be treated as formulation tools, not imported theorems.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","formal reduction","algorithmic formulation"],"summary":"Recast the fixed-envelope condition as a countably constrained zero-sum game: the learner chooses marginal laws, the adversary chooses horizons or horizon mixtures, and best responses are fixed quotes or finite-prefix dual certificates. Compare this to infinite-game ERM-oracle, fixed-design online learning, and predictable-sequence frameworks to identify assumptions that would imply gluing.","title":"Infinite-Game Fixed-Design Formulation","why_promising":"The web search surfaced adjacent fixed-design and infinite-game sources not yet in the bibliography. This may yield cleaner terminology and possibly algorithmic sufficient conditions between stable-comparator cases and the full compactness criterion."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
